{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "main": ".open-next/worker.js",
  "name": "akshar-byonyks-web",

  // THE PUBLIC ADDRESSES, declared here rather than clicked into the
  // dashboard (4 Sep 2026, when the domain went live on Cloudflare).
  //
  // `custom_domain: true` is not the same thing as a route. A route matches
  // requests against a hostname that must already resolve somewhere; a custom
  // domain CREATES the DNS record and provisions the certificate, which is
  // what a Worker needs, because a Worker has no origin IP for an A record to
  // point at. That is also why the Namecheap parking records had to be
  // deleted first: this cannot claim a hostname another record already holds.
  //
  // IN CONFIG RATHER THAN IN THE UI so the site's addresses are reviewable in
  // the diff and reproducible on a rebuild. A domain attached by hand exists
  // only in one account's dashboard and is invisible to anyone reading this
  // repository to find out where the site actually lives.
  //
  // BOTH HOSTNAMES SERVE THE SAME WORKER, and www is not redirected. Every
  // page sets an absolute canonical from `metadataBase` in `app/layout.tsx`,
  // which is `siteUrl` — the apex — so a page served at www still tells search
  // engines the apex is the real one and the two are consolidated. A 301 would
  // need its own proxied placeholder record for www to hang off, to do a job
  // the canonicals already do correctly.
  "routes": [
    { "pattern": "aksharbyonyks.com", "custom_domain": true },
    { "pattern": "www.aksharbyonyks.com", "custom_domain": true }
  ],

  // THE SECOND PUBLIC COPY OF THIS SITE, closed 4 Sep 2026 — once the real
  // domain above was confirmed serving, and not before.
  //
  // `workers_dev` DEFAULTS TO TRUE, so from the first deploy this worker also
  // answered on `akshar-byonyks-web.<subdomain>.workers.dev`: a complete,
  // public, crawlable second copy of a medical-device company's site at an
  // address nobody chose and nothing on the site links to. Every page does emit
  // an absolute canonical back to `aksharbyonyks.com` (via `metadataBase`), so
  // the duplicate-content risk was largely contained — but a canonical is a
  // hint, not a redirect, and the address remains linkable, shareable and
  // screenshot-able by anyone who has ever seen a build log. There is no reason
  // for it to exist now that the domain works.
  //
  // THE ORDER MATTERED. This stayed `true` deliberately while the nameservers
  // moved, because it was the only address that could serve the site if the
  // custom domain failed to provision. Turning it off before the apex was
  // confirmed would have been removing the safety net while still on the wire.
  //
  // `preview_urls` IS NOT AN INDEPENDENT DEFAULT, AND THIS IS THE TRAP. The
  // config schema advertises `default: false`, which is wrong in the way that
  // costs you something: Cloudflare documents Preview URLs as *inheriting*
  // `workers_dev` when not explicitly set — "enabled by default if workers_dev
  // is enabled, and disabled by default if it is disabled." So `workers_dev:
  // false` on its own would silently switch off the per-version preview URLs
  // that Workers Builds produces for non-production branches, and the branch
  // builds configured in the dashboard would upload versions with no address to
  // view them at. Declaring `true` here is what keeps that feature alive, and
  // it has to be explicit precisely because the inherited value would be false.
  //
  // PREVIEW URLS ARE PUBLIC BY DEFAULT. They are per-version
  // (`<version>-<name>.<subdomain>.workers.dev`) rather than a standing address,
  // but they are not secret. Cloudflare Access is what gates them; if its scope
  // is set to "All traffic" rather than "Previews only" it will also sit in
  // front of the production domain, which is not wanted here.
  "workers_dev": false,
  "preview_urls": true,

  "compatibility_date": "2026-08-15",
  "compatibility_flags": [
    "nodejs_compat",
    "global_fetch_strictly_public"
  ],
  "assets": {
    "directory": ".open-next/assets",
    "binding": "ASSETS"
  },
  "services": [
    {
      "binding": "WORKER_SELF_REFERENCE",
      "service": "akshar-byonyks-web"
    }
  ],
  "images": {
    "binding": "IMAGES"
  },

  // CPU TIME PER REQUEST IS THE ONE PLAN-RELEVANT NUMBER THIS PROJECT CANNOT
  // MEASURE LOCALLY, so it is recorded from the first deploy rather than
  // discovered after a 1102.
  //
  // MEASURED 12 SEP 2026, THEN RE-READ CORRECTLY THE SAME DAY. THE 10 MS
  // CEILING DOES APPLY -- this account is on the Workers FREE plan.
  //
  // This note first claimed the opposite ("not on the Free plan's 10 ms
  // ceiling"), reasoning backwards from the fact that requests spending
  // 50-300 ms of CPU were returning 200. They were, and they still do, because
  // Cloudflare documents per-isolate slack: "some built-in flexibility to
  // allow for cases where your Worker infrequently runs over the configured
  // limit." Clearing a limit on grace is not the same as not having one.
  //
  // MEASURED OVER 8 DAYS (4-12 Sep), 8,152 requests:
  //
  //   success             8,114 req   mean 91.4 ms   p50 27.7 ms   max 865 ms
  //   exceededResources      22 req   mean 14.6 ms   p50 11.9 ms   max  33 ms
  //
  // The mean successful request is NINE TIMES the 10 ms allowance.
  //
  // WHAT ACTUALLY BROKE, 11 Sep 2026: 22 requests returned "exceededResources"
  // in two bursts sixteen minutes apart, during a scrape from Tencent Cloud.
  // This file used to read their low CPU as proof of memory pressure -- they
  // burned LESS CPU than the successes beside them, so the theory went, they
  // cannot have been killed for CPU. That is backwards. They are the CHEAPEST
  // requests in the entire dataset precisely BECAUSE they were terminated
  // early, and their 11.9 ms median sits directly on the 10 ms line. Error
  // 1102 is Cloudflare's unified "exceeded resource limits" response, which is
  // why the outcome reads "exceededResources" and not "exceededCpu".
  //
  // SO THIS SITE RUNS OVER ITS PLAN LIMIT AND SURVIVES ON SLACK, which holds
  // until traffic spikes and the slack disappears -- exactly what 11 Sep was.
  // The cache fix in "open-next.config.ts" cut the median to 11.9 ms, which is
  // better and still over. A Next.js server runtime cannot be tuned under
  // 10 ms; startup CPU alone exceeds it. The two real exits are the $5/month
  // Workers Paid plan, or `output: "export"` so that page views never invoke
  // the worker at all.
  //
  // For the record: the Workers Free plan allows 10 ms of CPU per HTTP
  // request; Paid defaults to 30 s. Exceeding it returns Error 1102 and logs
  // the outcome as "exceededCpu". Cloudflare's stated average across all
  // Workers is 2.2 ms, but this worker serves prerendered pages through a full
  // Next.js server runtime (29 MB unbundled), so the average is not a safe
  // proxy.
  //
  // `invocation_logs` is what carries `cpuTime` and `wallTime`. Sampling stays
  // at 1 (every request) — this site's request volume is nowhere near a level
  // where log volume is a cost, and a sampled tail would hide exactly the rare
  // spike that matters.
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true,
    "logs": {
      "invocation_logs": true,
      "head_sampling_rate": 1
    }
  }
}
